      "content": "Building an autonomous AI agent does not mean running an infinite inference loop inside a persistent container. In production environments, an always-on agent is largely an illusion maintained through event-driven architecture where the system spends most of its operational lifetime asleep.\n\nThe architecture relies on five core primitives: an event inbox, precise timers, structured memory, permission boundaries, and notification dispatchers. Instead of burning compute polling for updates, the agent engine wakes up when incoming webhook events or cron triggers arrive, evaluates accumulated session transcripts, and executes its task run before serializing state back to storage.\n\nThis decoupled execution model solves two major engineering headaches: token runaway costs and context window pollution. By treating agent activity as discrete transactional runs rather than unbroken threads, teams can reliably prune message histories and enforce fine-grained authorization policies at each wake cycle.\n\nDesigning reliable agent systems is fundamentally an exercise in robust distributed state management rather than prompt manipulation."
