      "image": null,
      "content": "Calling new Linux kernel interfaces from user space remains surprisingly cumbersome when glibc lacks native system call wrappers. Without official wrappers, developers must fall back to invoking the raw syscall() interface, forfeiting compile-time parameter validation and errno translation.\n\nAt the Linux Plumbers Conference, glibc maintainers evaluated how to expose kernel capabilities rapidly without compromising the C library's strict ABI guarantees. While specialized libraries like liburing package system calls into user-friendly abstractions, glibc must balance direct system call parity against maintaining long-term backward compatibility across diverse architectures.\n\nWriting bare syscall() wrappers directly in production backend services introduces fragility around register clobbering, type sign-extension, and platform-specific ABI variations. Understanding how the standard library manages this boundary helps systems engineers navigate low-level asynchronous I/O and modern container APIs safely.\n\nReliable systems programming requires knowing where the C runtime ends and the raw kernel interface begins."
    },
    {
