      "content": "Most benchmarks evaluate AI agents on reactive task execution where a human explicitly supplies context and instructions. Real proactive agency requires something much harder: observing complex multi-tool environments in the background and deciding when to step in without creating useless alert fatigue.\n\nA new initiative benchmark evaluated 12 frontier models across 50 simulated enterprise workflows spanning CRMs, issue trackers, billing systems, and Slack. Testing revealed massive variances in decision thresholds, tool invocation rates, and execution costs, with top-performing models demanding almost 20 tool calls and 54 seconds of inference per task.\n\nBuilding proactive agentic systems requires balancing passive evidence collection against operational noise. If an autonomous agent lacks disciplined stopping criteria, it simply floods operational channels with low-signal suggestions, driving down user trust.\n\nThe real bottleneck for production AI teammates is not raw reasoning power, but knowing when to remain silent."
