      "date": "2026-11-13",
      "authors": [
        "deafbeef"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how an unread 27-byte telemetry response can silently exhaust file descriptors and crash Electron applications. You will gain insight into debugging subtle resource exhaustion issues using Linux core dumps and Chromium internals.",
      "hn_id": "50483656",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50483656",
      "image": null,
      "content": "An unread 27-byte HTTP response body can quietly bring an entire machine to its knees. When running desktop applications built on Chromium or Electron, ignoring tiny background responses can lead to silent resource exhaustion that evades standard logging.\n\nIn this debugging breakdown, an application repeatedly froze every couple of days without logging any errors. The culprit was a presence check telemetry call. The application fired the network request but never consumed the 27-byte response payload. Because the body stream was never drained or closed, Chromium held the underlying file descriptor open indefinitely.\n\nOver several days of continuous runtime, the process hit the Linux per-process file descriptor ceiling. When the renderer subsequently attempted an operation requiring an open descriptor, it crashed immediately with SIGILL. The crash triggered systemd-coredump, which attempted to write a 1.5 TiB core dump to disk, saturating NVMe I/O and creating system-wide latency spikes with load averages passing 40.\n\nAlways ensure that HTTP response bodies are explicitly drained or closed in your clients, even for tiny telemetry pings that appear harmless."
    },
