      "content": "Granting autonomous coding agents raw bash access without guard rails is a recipe for operational disaster, yet sandboxing command execution often introduces heavy runtime latency. BraKet provides an intriguing alternative by statically parsing and classifying shell command capabilities in microseconds.\n\nImplemented in both Rust and TypeScript, the library parses command strings against a curated dataset of over 900 root commands and 8,000 sub-command paths. In benchmarks on Linux x86-64, the Rust engine classifies single-command execution capabilities (such as filesystem read, write, or network access) in approximately 550 nanoseconds, while handling compound pipelines in under 3 microseconds.\n\nInstead of evaluating permissions through slow process virtualization or naive regular expressions that miss shell expansions, static capability extraction allows agent runtimes to determine risk tiers before issuing execve. It cleanly distinguishes safe observation commands from destructive writes at wire speed.\n\nFast static classification delivers a practical security primitive for engineering production agent guard rails without degrading interactive latency."
