      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50482101",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Floating point arithmetic has long been recognized as catastrophic for financial ledgers because binary fractions cannot accurately represent decimal fractions like one-tenth. Many transactional architectures compensate by adopting 64-bit integer values to represent minimal base denominations, such as cents.\n\nTigerBeetle decided that 64 bits are no longer sufficient for serious financial engines, transitioning their core accounting records to 128-bit unsigned integers. A standard unsigned 64-bit integer caps out around 18 quintillion units, which seems immense until a system must track fractional asset subdivisions, foreign currency scaling factors, or massive cumulative balances without overflow.\n\nBy committing to native 128-bit numeric fields, the storage engine guarantees strict mathematical precision across volatile multi-currency operations while completely bypassing floating-point errors. This architectural choice trade-offs marginal memory footprint for guaranteed invariant safety across high-throughput distributed state machines.\n\nDesigning financial primitives requires choosing numeric boundaries that eliminate overflow failure modes before they can ever reach the storage engine."
    },
