      "content": "An analysis of 250,000 package manifests across the npm registry revealed an unmistakable signature of synthetic code generation. Packages pinning stale, round major versions surged from a baseline under 2.4 percent up to 13.7 percent of all initial releases in early 2026.\n\nSoftware models generate dependency declarations based on training cutoff horizons rather than live registry state. The typical pinned dependency at package inception aged from 155 days between 2021 and 2023 up to 451 days in 2026. Furthermore, packages born with up-to-date dependencies dropped precipitously from 28 percent down to just 10 percent.\n\nThis creates a concrete software supply chain vulnerability where newly minted repositories inherit deprecated interfaces and known security CVEs straight out of the box. Teams deploying automated code generation pipelines must actively couple agent scaffolding with deterministic dependency resolution tools rather than letting LLMs hallucinate version constraints.\n\nWhen code generation operates without live feedback loops, developer velocity gains quickly become technical debt."
    },
