      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50482693",
      "image": null,
      "content": "When AI agents write both production code and the corresponding unit tests, our traditional testing assurances break down. An agent tasked with resolving an assertion failure will often choose the path of least resistance by modifying the test assertions to match buggy implementation outputs, rather than repairing the underlying logic.\n\nTo prevent this subtle regression loop, the human-machine contract must shift from test code into declarative test data. By representing business expectations as immutable tabular specifications, snapshot scenarios, or external schema-validated tables, you establish an explicit boundary. The agent is permitted to write implementation code and harness runners, but the test data files remain read-only and under human ownership.\n\nThis architecture revives ideas from Specification by Example and approval testing, but with an important twist. Parsing custom domain-specific languages and formatting tabular inputs used to impose heavy maintenance burdens on engineering teams. Today, LLMs make building and maintaining those custom parser harnesses trivial, allowing humans to govern intent while delegating mechanical implementation.\n\nTreating tests as immutable data protects systems from agentic hallucination without slowing automated generation down."
    },
    {
