      "authors": [
        "znano"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how timestamp oracles solve distributed event ordering and provide crash-safe contiguous sequences. You will learn how consensus engines guarantee strict monotonicity without holes across failovers.",
      "hn_id": "50481056",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50481056",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Ordering events across distributed shards is notoriously difficult. Wall clocks skew, logical clocks do not easily yield contiguous sequences, and conventional database auto-increment sequences fail to scale across independent partitions. Timestamp oracles solve this by serving strictly monotonic IDs, but many implementations drop contiguousness during failovers.\n\ntsoracle is an open-source Rust implementation that tackles both monotonic timestamps and strictly gapless sequences. When building financial ledgers or audit logs, a missing sequence number requires manual reconciliation. This engine guarantees that sequences remain contiguous without holes even across node crashes and leader failovers.\n\nThe durability model guarantees safety by performing an fsync on window state before handing out any ID lease. It abstracts the consensus layer using traits, shipping out of the box with drivers for both openraft and OmniPaxos. A built-in gRPC client handles request coalescing, leader discovery, and automatic reconnection.\n\nBuilding MVCC storage engines or multi-partition transactional logs becomes dramatically simpler when ordering guarantees are offloaded to an explicit, crash-safe oracle."
    },
