    {
      "title": "OWE-Bench evaluates agent capability on long-horizon engineering projects",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.mercor.com/blog/introducing-owe-bench/",
      "date": "2026-11-13",
      "authors": [
        "Jacob Cole",
        "Cindy Yang",
        "Aravind Selvan",
        "Daniel Babalola",
        "Bertie Vidgen"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how OWE-Bench measures frontier AI performance across complex engineering domains using continuous grading rather than binary pass-fail metrics.",
      "hn_id": "50478125",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50478125",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most existing agent benchmarks evaluate discrete, single-step tasks that fail to reflect the grind of real engineering projects. Testing an agent on writing a self-contained function does not capture what happens when an agent must optimize a GPU kernel or modify an aircraft simulation across multiple days.\n\nOWE-Bench introduces 26 long-horizon engineering and research tasks evaluated on continuous scoring scales rather than binary pass-fail checks. When an agent improves a codebase toward a target performance metric, it receives partial credit proportional to the actual speedup achieved. The evaluation exposes how brutally token-heavy autonomous engineering remains in practice.\n\nFrontier models struggle significantly when horizon length grows. The top-performing model on the leaderboard achieved only 54.6 percent completion while consuming an average of 211 million tokens per attempt. Smaller models dropped below 4 percent, indicating that reasoning degrades quickly when context windows turn into massive state histories.\n\nEvaluating agents on continuous progress across multi-million token workflows will likely replace toy pull request benchmarks as the real test for production developer agents."
