    {
      "title": "Publishing breaking Rust changes without requiring coordinated downstream upgrades",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/dtolnay/semver-trick",
      "date": "2026-11-13",
      "authors": [
        "dtolnay"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how a library can depend on a newer version of itself to ship breaking changes smoothly. You will learn how to prevent painful ecosystem-wide upgrade coordination in Rust dependencies.",
      "hn_id": "50479984",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50479984",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Ecosystem-wide upgrades in typed languages often stall because two libraries transitively depend on incompatible versions of the same shared crate. In Rust, this historically triggered massive upgrade deadlocks like the libc transition, where consumers could not pass types between mismatched versions.\n\nThe semver trick solves this without requiring an all-at-once coordinated migration. When releasing version 0.3 with breaking changes, you publish an intermediate 0.2.1 release that re-exports types directly from 0.3. Downstream crates pinned to 0.2 now compile against identical type definitions under the hood.\n\nThis compatibility bridge works even for foreign function interfaces and complex traits. By letting the compiler treat types from disparate major versions as physically identical, you eliminate dependency diamond traps without breaking API contracts.\n\nDesigning libraries for smooth deprecation cycles is just as important as the feature set itself."
